title: Components that draw themselves
intro: Any component, a script's as much as Griff's, can now draw itself and say what it sends in a network game. The renderer no longer keeps a list of what draws.
project: Platformer | Halo Heights

chapter: A script that draws | A halo for the hero
say: Griff's sprites, tilemaps, bars and boards all draw themselves now, through one small interface. A script can use the same one.
write: Scripts/Halo.cs | drawable-components/Halo.cs
code: Assets/Scripts/Halo.cs | Halo.cs
say: Halo is an ordinary script that also says it's drawable. Its Draw method adds a ring and a label around its entity, the way a sprite adds its picture.
say: Its Pulse is a value only the running game keeps. Marking it Sent means a network game sends it to every player, just like a saved property.
hidecode
select: Player
add: Halo
explain: scene | Drawn in the Scene view | The ring shows straight away in the Scene view, drawn from the same quads the game draws.
set: Halo.Radius = 1.4
say: Change its radius in the Inspector and the ring follows.

chapter: In the game | Drawing in place, and over the world
play
wait: 1.5
explain: game | Pulsing round the player | In the game the ring pulses, and goes wherever the player goes.
hold: Right 1.5
say: The red marks come from a game system. Its Draw runs once every entity has drawn itself, and adds marks over the world, under the HUD. It's the same way Griff draws a strategy game's fog or a tactics game's reachable cells.
wait: 1
stop
say: Drawing and networking are open to every component now, so a component you write draws and plays online the same way Griff's own do.
